.TH LATENCY-COLLECTOR 8 2026-09-21 "Linux 7.0.9" "System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
latency-collector \- collect traces of closely occurring kernel latencies from the ftrace latency tracers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B latency-collector
.RI [ option ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B latency-collector
uses one of the kernel's ftrace latency tracers
.RB ( preemptirqsoff ,
.BR preemptoff ,
.BR irqsoff ,
.BR wakeup ,
.B \%wakeup_rt
or
.BR \%wakeup_dl )
and prints the contents of the ftrace
.I trace
file to standard output each time the tracer records a latency.
It detects a new latency by watching the
.I \%tracing_max_latency
file with
.BR inotify (7).
It runs until it receives
.BR SIGINT ,
.B SIGTERM
or
.BR SIGHUP .
.PP
Unless
.BR \-x ,
.B \-i
or
.B \-m
is given,
.B latency-collector
configures ftrace itself.
At start it saves the contents of the files
.I \%current_tracer
and
.IR \%tracing_thresh ,
and the state of the
.B function\-trace
and
.B display\-graph
options in
.IR \%trace_options .
If a tracer other than
.B nop
is active, it prints a message and exits, unless
.B \-F
is given, in which case it switches to the
.B nop
tracer.
It then writes 0 to
.IR \%tracing_max_latency ,
sets the options and the threshold, and selects the tracer.
When it receives
.BR SIGINT ,
.B SIGTERM
or
.BR SIGHUP ,
it prints the maximum latency recorded, read from
.IR \%tracing_max_latency ,
restores the saved tracer, threshold and options, and exits.
.PP
At start,
.B latency-collector
prints its settings, and each of its threads prints its thread ID,
scheduling policy and priority.
The process runs with the
.B \%SCHED_RR
policy at priority 99 unless
.B \-c
or
.B \-p
is given.
Detected latencies are numbered from 1 and handed to a pool of print
threads.
Each printout starts with a line of the form
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
\fIseconds\fP.\fImicroseconds\fP Latency \fIN\fP immediate print
.EE
.in
.PP
where the time is taken from
.BR \%CLOCK_MONOTONIC ,
followed by the contents of the trace file between lines marked
.B BEGIN
and
.BR END .
With random sleeping (see
.BR \-r ),
the first line reads
.RI \(dq "randomly sleep for " T " ms before print" \(dq
instead when the thread slept before reading the trace file, and a line
.RI \(dq "Latency " N " printout skipped due to random delay" \(dq
is printed when another latency was detected during the sleep.
Detected latencies wait in a queue for a free print thread; if the queue
is full, a warning is printed and the latency is not printed.
With random sleeping, a latency is also not printed if it is detected
while a trace is being printed, or if another latency has been chosen for
printing between its detection and the time a print thread takes it; with
.B \-v
given twice, a message is printed for each such latency.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-list
List the latency tracers from the list above that the running kernel
supports, and exit.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-tracer " \fItracer\fP"
Use
.IR tracer .
By default the first tracer supported by the kernel is used, in this
order: preemptirqsoff, preemptoff, irqsoff, wakeup, \%wakeup_rt, \%wakeup_dl.
A tracer name that is not one of these produces a warning;
a tracer that the kernel does not support is an error.
.TP
.BR \-F ", " \-\-force
Start even if a tracer other than
.B nop
is active.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-threshold " \fIthreshold\fP"
Write
.I threshold
(in microseconds, decimal digits only) to
.IR \%tracing_thresh ,
so that the tracer records every latency longer than
.IR threshold .
The default is 0, with which the tracer records a latency only if it is
longer than the value in
.IR \%tracing_max_latency ,
the longest latency recorded so far.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-function
Enable the
.B function\-trace
option in
.IR \%trace_options ,
so that ftrace also traces the functions executed during a latency.
Without it only the beginning, the end and a backtrace are recorded.
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-graph
Enable the
.B display\-graph
option in
.IR \%trace_options ,
so that ftrace shows how the traced functions call each other.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-policy " \fIpolicy\fP"
Run with the scheduling policy
.IR policy ,
one of
.BR other ,
.BR batch ,
.BR idle ,
.B rr
or
.BR fifo .
The default is
.BR rr .
Unless
.B \-p
is given, the priority is 99 for
.B rr
and
.B fifo
and 0 for the other policies.
The
.B rr
and
.B fifo
policies may cause latencies for other tasks.
An unknown policy prints a warning and the usage message and exits.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-priority " \fIpriority\fP"
Run with the scheduling priority
.I priority
(decimal digits only).
A priority outside the range of the policy is raised to its minimum or
lowered to its maximum, with a message.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-notrace
Do not print the trace file when a latency is detected; print only a
line
.RI \(dq "Latency " N " detected" \(dq.
This also turns random sleeping off.
.BR \-r ,
.B \-a
and
.B \-u
given after
.B \-n
turn trace printing back on.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-threads " \fIcount\fP"
Use
.I count
print threads (decimal digits only, at least 1).
The default is 3 and the maximum 40; a larger value is reduced to 40 with
a warning.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-random
Before reading the trace file, sleep with a certain probability for the
time set by
.B \-u
(default 1000 ms).
Opening the trace file causes a blackout of hundreds of milliseconds in
which the latency tracer notes no latencies; sleeping at random avoids
always collecting the first of a cluster of closely occurring latencies.
If another latency is detected while a thread sleeps, the sleeping
thread skips its printout so that the later latency can be printed
instead.
The probabilities are chosen so that each latency of a cluster is equally
likely to be the one collected; see
.BR \-a .
Random sleeping is intended for the irqsoff, preemptoff and
preemptirqsoff tracers; with another tracer a warning is printed.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-nrlat " \fInrlat\fP"
Assume, for the random sleep probabilities, that a cluster contains at
most
.I nrlat
latencies (decimal digits only, at least 1; default 2).
Implies
.BR \-r .
The first latency of a cluster is printed without sleeping with
probability 1/\fInrlat\fP, the second with probability
1/(\fInrlat\fP\-1), and so on, up to probability 1 for latency number
.IR nrlat .
When that last probability is used,
.I nrlat
is increased by one; with
.B \-v
a message is printed each time.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-time " \fItime\fP"
Sleep for
.I time
milliseconds (decimal digits only) when random sleeping decides to
sleep.
The default is 1000.
Implies
.BR \-r .
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Given once, print a message when
.I nrlat
is increased and when ftrace settings are changed or restored.
Given twice or more, also print a message for each latency that random
sleeping causes not to be printed.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-no\-ftrace
Do not configure ftrace: the tracer, the threshold and the options are
left as they are,
.I \%tracing_max_latency
is not reset, and nothing is restored or reported at exit.
The tracer is then expected to have been set up through the files in the
tracing directory.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-tracefile " \fIfile\fP"
Print
.I file
instead of the ftrace
.I trace
file.
Implies
.BR \-x .
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-max\-lat " \fIfile\fP"
Watch
.I file
instead of the ftrace
.I \%tracing_max_latency
file.
Implies
.BR \-x .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage message, which includes the default file paths, and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B latency-collector
exits with status 0 when it terminates on
.BR SIGINT ,
.B SIGTERM
or
.BR SIGHUP ,
after
.B \-h
or
.BR \-l ,
and also after printing the usage message for an unknown option or an
invalid
.BR \-c ,
.B \-e
or
.B \-a
argument, and when it refuses to start because another tracer is
active.
It exits with status 1 on other errors, for example an argument that is
not a decimal number, an unsupported tracer, a missing tracing directory,
a failure to set the scheduling policy, or a failure to read or write a
tracing file.
.SH FILES
The tracing directory is located through libtracefs; it is normally
.IR \%/sys/kernel/tracing .
.TP
.I trace
The trace that is printed for each latency.
.TP
.I \%tracing_max_latency
Watched with inotify to detect latencies.
Unless
.B \-x
is in effect, it is set to 0 at start and read at exit.
.TP
.I \%current_tracer
.PD 0
.TP
.I \%tracing_thresh
.TP
.I \%trace_options
.PD
Saved at start, changed, and restored at exit, unless
.B \-x
is in effect.
.TP
.I \%available_tracers
Read to find the tracers that the kernel supports.
.TP
.I \%/dev/urandom
Read to seed the random number generator of each print thread.
If it cannot be read, the thread's index is used as the seed and a
warning is printed.
.SH NOTES
The irqsoff and preemptoff tracers need a kernel built with
.B \%CONFIG_IRQSOFF_TRACER
and
.B \%CONFIG_PREEMPT_TRACER
respectively; preemptirqsoff needs both.
The wakeup, \%wakeup_rt and \%wakeup_dl tracers need
.BR \%CONFIG_SCHED_TRACER .
.PP
.B latency-collector
needs read and write access to the files in the tracing directory, which
is normally restricted to privileged users.
Setting the
.B rr
or
.B fifo
policy requires the
.B \%CAP_SYS_NICE
capability (on Peios,
.BR \%SeIncreaseBasePriorityPrivilege ),
unless the
.B \%RLIMIT_RTPRIO
resource limit permits the priority; if the policy cannot be set,
.B latency-collector
exits.
.PP
The
.B function\-trace
and
.B display\-graph
options are changed only if they appear in
.IR \%trace_options ;
otherwise
.B \-f
and
.B \-g
have no effect.
.PP
If
.B latency-collector
is terminated by a signal other than
.BR SIGINT ,
.B SIGTERM
or
.BR SIGHUP ,
the ftrace settings it changed are not restored.
.SH EXAMPLES
Collect traces with the irqsoff tracer, including function traces, and
choose at random among clustered latencies:
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
latency-collector \-t irqsoff \-f \-r
.EE
.in
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR inotify (7),
.BR sched (7)
